1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:05,920
Since we will look into whether 2 or more 
concurrent threads are correctly handling  

2
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:10,400
shared variables, let's introduce a system 
model for the critical section problem. 

3
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:15,760
From now on, we assume each of 
our threads looks like this: 

4
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:20,800
we have a loop that runs forever (it's a 
model, of course it will stop in practice,  

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,120
but from the model perspective we do not 
assume anything about when it will stop) 

6
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:31,760
inside the loop, we have a critical section, 
the one in which we read/write shared variables, 

7
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:38,160
we assume shared variables are accessed with 
atomic read/write operations, so only one read  

8
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:44,240
or write operation can be performed at a time
before the critical section, we have an entry  

9
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:51,600
section, a statement/line/multiple lines whose 
execution implies the critical section can start:  

10
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:57,680
you can see the entry section as the portion in 
which the thread is trying to acquire a LOCK,  

11
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:03,360
its right to enter its critical section
after the critical section, we have an exit  

12
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:09,760
section, a statement/line/multiple lines whose 
execution implies the critical section is over:  

13
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:14,800
you can see the exit section as the portion 
in which the thread releases its LOCK,  

14
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:22,080
for someone else to enter their critical section
and a remainder section, basically the rest  

15
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:26,320
of the code in which we do not worry 
about reading/writing shared variables. 

16
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:31,760
Notice that for simplicity we also 
assume threads' execution does not fail! 

17
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:38,400
Our goal of having 2 or more 
concurrent threads correctly handling  

18
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,880
shared variables can then be formulated as: 

19
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:47,840
Define an entry and an exit section that ensure:
Mutual exclusion, 

20
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:54,960
Progress (i.e., no deadlock/no livelock), and
Fairness (e.g., bounded waiting or no starvation). 

21
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:02,400
Mutual exclusion means that only one thread 
at a time can execute its critical section. 

22
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:07,840
Progress means that if no thread 
is in its critical section and one  

23
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:14,640
is/some are trying to enter their, then one 
must enter its critical section in bounded time. 

24
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:18,640
Notice that we do not make any assumption about  

25
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:23,040
the duration of the reminder section of 
a thread. A thread can stay there for  

26
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:27,840
as long as it needs. The duration of the 
reminder section is NOT bounded in time.

27
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:36,320
Let's put also a temporary extra assumption 
for now: we only have 2 concurrent threads.

28
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:45,440
If we want to visualize such 
behavior it looks like this figure. 

29
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:48,880
As you can see, only one thread 
at a time can enter the critical  

30
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:56,400
region. If a thread B tries to enter it when 
another thread A is in it, then B waits.

31
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:04,080
What we are going to do next is to look 
at various ways of defining the entry  

32
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:12,000
and exit section and ask ourselves: does solution 
X ensure mutual exclusion, progress, and fairness?

33
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:19,760
We want formal correctness proofs. We cannot 
answer with "it looks like it does!" nor with  

34
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:25,440
"I tried running the code 1000000 times and 
it never broke, so I am confident it does".

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,200
To provide a formal answer, we are going to 
try to find one execution of the threads that  

36
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:37,600
breaks one of the properties. If we find it, then 
the solution is not valid (it breaks one of the  

37
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:45,840
desired properties). If we show such an execution 
does not exist, then the solution is valid.

38
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:51,520
Let's try to look at a specific (simple) 
example, which we refer to as check-completion.

39
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:56,000
Let's say the code of our 2 
threads, A and B, looks like this. 

40
00:03:56,640 --> 00:04:00,080
They both share 2 boolean 
variables, flagA and flagB,  

41
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:06,080
initially set to false, which they use to indicate 
their intention to enter the critical section.

42
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,320
Here I show A's code. B's code is symmetric. 

43
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:17,120
In its entry section, A waits as long 
as B's flag is true in a while loop. 

44
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:23,360
This is called busywait because A is 
using CPU cycles to spin in a loop. 

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,200
The loop ends when B sets its flag to false.
After that, A sets its flag to true,  

46
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:36,160
enters the critical section, and when the 
critical section is over sets its flag to false. 

47
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:41,840
Is this solution to the critical 
section problem valid or not? 

48
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:48,160
I strongly suggest you stop the video and 
try to reason and provide a formal answer.

49
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:56,720
Now let's try to answer the question together.
That is, let's try to find an example  

50
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:03,120
showing one of the properties can be violated.
Can the property of mutual exclusion be violated? 

51
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:08,800
For instance, can A enter its critical 
section while B is in its critical section? 

52
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:13,280
Let's answer the question 
with the help of this drawing. 

53
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:17,440
In this drawing, we see that A 
enters its critical section after B  

54
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:22,320
entered its own but before B 
exited it. Can this happen?.

55
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:31,040
To happen, sometime before entering its 
critical section A needs to set its flag  

56
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:36,800
to true (that's what the code says).
And before that, A needs to see that  

57
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:43,520
the value of flagB is false to exit the busywait 
while loop (again, that's what the code says). 

58
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:49,520
Now let's focus on B. To enter its 
critical section, B needs to set  

59
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:56,960
flagB to true (that's what the code says).
Now we are at a critical point. In our example, if  

60
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:08,080
we assume B sets its flagB to true before A sees 
flagB's value is false, then it means in between  

61
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:14,880
these two events B completes its critical section, 
otherwise A cannot see flagB being false...

62
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:23,120
In the example we are studying, the point 
in which B sets flagB to true comes after  

63
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:29,280
A sees flagB value to be false.
So now we are left with one question: can B  

64
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:38,560
see flagA being false in between the time A 
sees flagB to be false and B sets flagB to true? 

65
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:45,200
The answer is yes because flagA is 
initially set to false and is also set to  

66
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:52,240
false every time A exits its critical section.
So, in the figure, the portion of time during  

67
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:59,120
which A has not yet entered its critical section 
is a period during which flagA's value is false. 

68
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:05,760
We have found an example that breaks the 
mutual exclusion, which implies the code  

69
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:09,120
is NOT a solution to the critical section problem.

70
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:14,960
Once you listen to this argumentation, 
you "digest" it and you understand it,  

71
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:20,800
you will probably agree that proving whether a 
solution to the critical problem is valid or not  

72
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:25,520
is very challenging. In all the following 
videos, I strongly suggest you stop  

73
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:29,840
and try to formally find out yourself 
whether a solution is correct or not!

